WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.551
Directory permissions are part of file
permission security.

2
00:00:03.631 --> 00:00:06.271
A file's own permissions are only part of
the picture.

3
00:00:06.351 --> 00:00:10.048
Directory access can affect whether a file
can be found, created, removed,

4
00:00:10.128 --> 00:00:11.243
renamed, or replaced.

5
00:00:11.323 --> 00:00:15.489
In some environments, directory search
permission can matter even when individual

6
00:00:15.569 --> 00:00:17.388
file permissions look restrictive.

7
00:00:17.468 --> 00:00:19.580
Shared locations require careful design.

8
00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.767
Upload directories, export folders, cache
directories, temporary directories,

9
00:00:23.847 --> 00:00:27.661
and shared service areas may be touched by
multiple processes or identities.

10
00:00:27.741 --> 00:00:31.320
The application needs clear rules for
naming, ownership, permissions,

11
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.627
and cleanup so files do not accidentally
cross trust boundaries.

12
00:00:34.707 --> 00:00:38.873
A directory that permits broad creation or
replacement can weaken the protection of

13
00:00:38.953 --> 00:00:39.774
files inside it.

14
00:00:39.854 --> 00:00:42.553
If another process can create names,
replace entries,

15
00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:44.452
or remove files in a shared location,

16
00:00:44.532 --> 00:00:47.231
the application must account for that in
its workflow.

17
00:00:47.311 --> 00:00:51.008
File permissions and directory permissions
should be designed together.

18
00:00:51.088 --> 00:00:54.315
Cleanup routines deserve the same caution
as creation routines.

19
00:00:54.395 --> 00:00:58.385
Cleanup should delete or replace only
files inside the intended managed area and

20
00:00:58.465 --> 00:00:59.932
only files the workflow owns.

21
00:01:00.012 --> 00:01:02.359
Vague paths, broad matching, unchecked
names,

22
00:01:02.439 --> 00:01:06.546
or assumptions about the current directory
can turn cleanup into a reliability and

23
00:01:06.626 --> 00:01:07.565
security problem.

24
00:01:07.645 --> 00:01:11.048
Good directory design shapes what the
process and other users can do.

25
00:01:11.128 --> 00:01:13.885
It gives each workflow an appropriate
place to operate,

26
00:01:13.965 --> 00:01:17.369
limits unnecessary access, and makes
permission review practical.

27
00:01:17.449 --> 00:01:20.441
Instead of relying on every call site to
remember every rule,

28
00:01:20.521 --> 00:01:23.220
the directory layout should embody part of
the policy.
